<p><strong>Honest gaps, stated plainly:</strong> the Iowa-tailored resume documents formal WCAG training and routine JAWS/NVDA testing plus axe remediation, but names no accessibility certification (for example, IAAP WAS/CPACC or DHS Trusted Tester), and none is claimed here. What this portfolio does show is hands-on WCAG 2.1 AA implementation and a documented axe + JAWS testing workflow, so an interviewer can evaluate the actual skill rather than a credential. Likewise, no degree in Human-Computer Interaction is claimed; the author's degrees are the MS and BTech in IT above, plus 10+ years of professional experience.</p>

<h2>For interviewers</h2>
<p>Good discussion starters in this codebase: why validation lives in the Application layer rather than only in the controller; how the design-system tokens would let a second team adopt the components without copying CSS; what the legacy WebForms sample gets wrong by modern standards and what to keep when modernizing it; and where automated accessibility checks stop and manual JAWS testing must begin. Each is documented in <code>docs/</code> and linked from the <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="CodeSamples">Code samples page</a>.</p>

<h2>Run it locally</h2>
<pre><code>dotnet run --project src/IowaPortal.Web
# then open http://localhost:5000 (the exact port is printed on startup)
# Tests: dotnet test</code></pre>
<p>Full prerequisites, configuration, SQL Server setup, and troubleshooting are in the README at the repository root (included in the download). No secrets, accounts, or cloud services are required to run the demo.</p>
